 if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186262802 1071 "Calculate $det(D)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle D=\\begin{bmatrix}2x & y \\\\ -5 & xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186262803 1071 "If\n    $$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186262803 1071 "If\n$$\n\\displaystyle U=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -5 \\\\ 2k & -k+2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nand\n$\\text{det}(U)=-1$, what is the value of $k?$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265899 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children”; “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots”; “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card”." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265899 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of children who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected students can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $200$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of three-point shots in $15$ shots can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $15$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75\\%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of purchases made with a credit card in $10$ purchases can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of students who like their teacher among $200$ randomly selected primary school children”; “The number of three-point shots made by a basketball player in 15 shots”; “The number of purchases made with a credit card in a store out of $10$ observed purchases, if, on average, $75\\%$ of all purchases are made with a credit card”." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265952 1071 "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265952 1071 "At an ice cream shop, on average, $25\\%$ of customers buy chocolate ice cream. What is the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first person buying a chocolate ice cream? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265952 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265952 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of customers that enter the ice cream shop until the first one buys a chocolate ice cream. Since the chance of a customer buying a chocolate ice cream is $25\\%,$ the number of customers before the first buys a chocolate ice cream can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=25\\%=\\frac{25}{100}=0.25.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.25).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.25)^{x-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{x-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the $4$th customer of the day is the first buying a chocolate ice cream is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=4) & =f(4) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{4-1}(0.25) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{3}(0.25) \\\\  & ≈0.105\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265969 1071 "In a certain school, $17.5%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265969 1071 "In a certain school, $17.5\\%$ of students are left-handed. In a random sample of $8$ students from this school, what is the probability that at least $6$ students are left-handed? Round your answer to $4$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265977 1071 "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265977 1071 "A coin is being randomly picked from a jar and put back in until getting a one-dollar coin. The jar holds a $70\\%$ of one-dollar coins. What is the probability that a one-dollar coin is chosen before the $5$th trial?  Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265977 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265977 1074 "A geometric random variable $X$ with probability of success $p$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=(1-p)^{x-1}p\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of choices before choosing a one-dollar coin. Since the chance of choosing a one-dollar coin is $70\\%,$ the number of choices required to choose a one-dollar coin can be modeled as a geometric random variable with a probability of success\n\n$$\np=70\\%=\\frac{70}{100}=0.7.\n$$\n\nSo, $X∼\\text{Geom}(0.7).$ In this case, $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-0.7)^{x-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{x-1}(0.7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of choosing a one-dollar coin before the $5$th trial is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<5) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3,4}) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4) \\\\  & =(0.3)^{1-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{2-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3-1}(0.7)+(0.3)^{4-1}(0.7) \\\\  & =(0.7)+(0.3)(0.7)+(0.3)^{2}(0.7)+(0.3)^{3}(0.7) \\\\  & =[1+0.3+(0.3)^{2}+(0.3)^{3}](0.7) \\\\  & ≈0.992\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265999 1071 "A basketball player has a $70%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265999 1071 "A basketball player has a $70\\%$ success rate in shooting from the free throw line during a season. The player has ten shots from the free throw line in one match. What is the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186265999 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186265999 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be the number of successful free throws.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ shots from the free throw line in the match\n\n- the probability that the player is successful from the free throw line is $p=70\\%=0.7$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.7).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the player is successful with at most eight throws. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 8).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 8)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 8) & =1-P(X>8) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=9)+P(X=10)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{9})(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{10-9}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{10})(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{10-10}] \\\\  & =1-[10(0.7)^{9}(0.3)^{1}+1(0.7)^{10}(0.3)^{0}] \\\\  & =1-0.149\\,308 \\\\  & ≈0.851\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186268917 1071 "A cinema admission fee is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ dollars and a standard deviation of $$0.75.$ Ad