\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+6 \\\\  & =-0.5(10)+6 \\\\  & =-5+6 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, according to this model, the motorcycle's value $10$ years after it was purchased is around $$1\\,000.$\n\nNote: The point where $x=10$ is not shown on our trend line. Therefore, our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186268941 1074 "First, using the hint, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot.\n\n![](images/14/145a6a57bcd24c99e7de73f470de61c673b10f5a0d88b0c64527a17e52a0dc86.png)\n\nThe trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,5)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,4).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-5}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to calculate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+b \\\\ 5 & =-0.5(2)+b \\\\ 5 & =-1+b \\\\ b & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-0.5x+6.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x=10$ into the equation above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0.5x+