1 "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186302180 1071 "The manufacturing times of bicycle frames are normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ minutes and a standard deviation of $5$ minutes. Additionally, $25\\%$ of frames are completed in under $48$ minutes. Find the percentage of frames that take longer than $52$ minutes. **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ | $\\,0.5557\\,$ | $\\,0.5596\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ | $\\,0.5948\\,$ | $\\,0.5987\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ | $\\,0.6331\\,$ | $\\,0.6368\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186302180 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186302180 1074 "Let $X$ represent the time required to manufacture a bicycle frame. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{5}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>52).$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<48)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<48) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{48-\\mu }{5}\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ \\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)=-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{48-\\mu }{5} & =-0.6745 \\\\ 48-\\mu  & =-3.3725 \\\\ \\mu  & =51.3725.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(52<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>52) & =P(Z>\\frac{52-51.3725}{5}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.13) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.13) \\\\  & =1-0.5517 \\\\  & ≈44.83\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $44.83\\%$ of the frames take longer than $52$ minutes." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186302182 1071 "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186302182 1071 "The resting heart rate of a group of adults is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and a standard deviation of $8\\,\\text{bpm}.$ Additionally, $70\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate between $0.9\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}$ and $1.1\\mu \\,\\text{bpm}.$ Find the percentage of adults with a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0359\\,$ | $\\,0.0351\\,$ | $\\,0.0344\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0446\\,$ | $\\,0.0436\\,$ | $\\,0.0427\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0548\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186302182 1074 "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186302182 1074 "Let $X$ represent the resting heart rate of a randomly selected adult. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{8}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<70).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu )=0.70.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.9\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.70 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.35 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.5+0.35 \\\\ P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.1\\mu ) & =0.85 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}) & =0.85 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8}\\right) & =0.85 \\\\ \\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.85).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.85)=1.0364.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.1\\mu }{8} & =1.0364 \\\\ 0.1\\mu  & =8.2912 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{8.2912}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈82.912.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<70)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<70) & =P(Z<\\frac{70-82.912}{8}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.61) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.61) \\\\  & =0.0537 \\\\  & ≈5.37\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $5.37\\%$ of the adults have a resting heart rate lower than $70\\,\\text{bpm}.$" 13194139539466 175921865764