d the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186326903 1071 "The relationship between the advertising budget and sales (both in millions of dollars) of a company per year over several years is shown in the following table. \n\n| Advertising $(x)$ | **$2$** | **$2.5$** | **$3$** | **$3.5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Sales $(y)$ | $10$ | $12$ | $16$ | $20$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the total sales when the annual advertising budget is equal to $\\$5$ million, assuming the trend continues? \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{X}^{T}y=\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186326903 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\hat{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}31.5 & -11 \\\\ -11 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}58 \\\\ 168\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4.2 \\\\ 6.8\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=-4.2+6.8x.$ \n\nFinally, to predict the value of the sales when the advertising budget is equal to $$5$ million, we substitute $x=5$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4.2+6.8(5) \\\\  & =29.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the total sales when the advertising budget equals $$5$ million will be $$29.8$ million. \n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=5$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186326903 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\hat{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2.5 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3.5\\end{bmatrix}}^{X}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{bmatrix}}^{\\beta } & =\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 16 \\\\ 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{y},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\hat{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\hat{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\