nsists of $4$ rectangular faces. \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area of the sides} & =4\\times (\\text{Area of the rectangle}) \\\\  & =4\\times 28 \\\\  & =112\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the cost to paint the surface is $\\$6.50$ per square foot, the total amount that Chloe will have to pay is\n\n$$\n112\\times 6.50=$728.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186332971 1071 "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186332971 1071 "In a particular business bank account, the rate at which money is being deposited into the account is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=600(2t+1)^{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in dollars per month, and $t>0$ is the number of months since the account was opened. When the account was first opened, the business owners placed an initial deposit of $\\$1\\,000$ into the account.\n\nHow much money will be in the account after $3$ months?" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186332971 1074 "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186332971 1074 "We're given a **rate of change** in the amount of money $r(t).$ \n\nTo find the **total amount** of money in the account after $t$ months (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}r(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{t}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo be sure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nWe now let $t=3,$ and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =1\\,000+{\\int }_{0}^{3}600(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+600{\\int }_{0}^{3}(2x+1)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1\\,000+\\frac{600}{2\\cdot 4}{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75{(2t+1)^{4}|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[(2(3)+1)^{4}-(2(0)+1)^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[{7}^{4}-{1}^{4}] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,401-1] \\\\  & =1\\,000+75[2\\,400] \\\\  & =181\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there will be $\\$181\\,000$ in the account after $3$ months." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186335991 1071 "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n    $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186335991 1071 "Calculate $det(H)$, if\n$$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2r & 3s \\\\ -2t & 3u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186344995 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot x+(-4)\\cdot y \\\\ (-3)\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Notice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y \\\\ -3x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation  $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186344995 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0\\cdot x+(-4)\\cdot y \\\\ (-3)\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Notice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4y \\\\ -3x+y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation  $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186348354 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186348354 1074 "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y \\\\ 1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2x-y \\\\ x-y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186351475 1074 "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186351475 1074 "First, rewrite the order of the variables in the usual way:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12x+5y \\\\ x+3y\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-12 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186354331 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of members of a school band” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65%$”; “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45%$” only." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186354331 1074 "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The number of members of a school band” could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of school band members cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65\\%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of goals in $10$ penalties can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $10$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45\\%$” could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $5$ matches can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $5$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The number of goals scored by a soccer player in $10$ penalties if the probability of scoring any penalty is $65\\%$”; “The number of victories of a chess player in $5$ matches if the probability of the player winning any individual game is $45\\%$” only." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186354342 1071 "A painter at an exhibition has a $35%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186354342 1071 "A painter at an exhibition has a $35\\%$ chance of selling each of his works of art. What is the probability that when exhibiting $14$ works of art, the painter sells $2$ or more? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186354342 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 false] [17592186354342 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of works of art sold.\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=14$ works of art\n\n- the probability of selling each work is $p=35\\%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(14,0.35).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(1-0.35)^{14-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{x})(0.35)^{x}(0.65)^{14-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the painter sells $2$ or more works of art. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 2).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2)=P(X=2)+P(X=3)+…+P(X=14)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 2) & =1-P(X<2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{0})(0.35)^{0}(0.65)^{14-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{14}{1})(0.35)^{1}(0.65)^{14-1}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.65)^{14}+14(0.35)(0.65)^{13}] \\\\  & =1-0.020\\,519 \\\\  & =0.979\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539466 17592186576411 true] [17592186354535 1071 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=